On the inventory the question sets up Au Ag Cu Sn Pb Fe Hg + S, Zn and Sb being put aside by the terms of the question, the Odyssey’s named material vocabulary on the received text is:

* chrysos = Au, e.g. Od. 4.73, 7.85 ff., 18.295;
* argyros = Ag, e.g. Od. 4.62, 7.86, 4.73;
* chalkos = the heroic hard metal, the copper-base bronze of the age, Od. 1.264, 8.392, 11.32 etc. Functionally the Homeric copper/bronze substance; pure Cu is never named apart from in the alloy name;
* theion = S, brimstone, in the ritual purification after the slaughter of the Suitors;
* molybdos/molubdos = Pb on the normal lexicographical reading of the received text, Od. 10.19, 24.231, the Aeolus seal and the door-scene;
* sideros = Fe on the received text, Od. 9.392, 21.13-14, 24.241, the normal scholarly citation even if text-critically marginal.

Carbon is not an archaic elemental category. Charcoal/soot/ash are carbonaceous materials in modern chemical terms but elemental carbon was not known as an isolable substance in the archaic inventory and the poem does not name carbon as an element. Sulfur is different because theion/brimstone is actually named and used.

With that received reading the classical seven-metal-plus-sulfur set is represented in name by Au, Ag, chalkos/Cu, Pb, Fe and S.

The two members of the classical inventory that have no Homeric name-form at all in the Odyssey under any standard are the canonical post-Homeric metals:

* kassiteros/kassiteron = Sn, tin. The Greek name for tin is a Phoenician loan first securely Greek in Hes. WD 536 and later prose. In the Odyssey bronze is always chalkos; the white metal which makes the bronze is present only implicitly in the alloy. Tin is never given a separate Homeric name.
* hydrargyros/argyrion rheon = Hg, quicksilver/mercury. The liquid metal known in Egypt and the Near East is completely unnamed in the epics. The first secure Greek names for it are 5th c. and later. Hermes the god is there, the metal ‘water-silver’ is not.

Those are the uncontested, robustly post-Homeric omissions. Au and Ag are unambiguous elemental names, theion names sulfur and chalkos covers the Homeric copper/bronze material.

Remark on the residual dispute. If molybdos is rejected on an ultra-strict text-critical nominalism, Pb joins Sn and Hg as a classical metal without an independent secure Homeric name-form, giving the strict-text-critical absent set lead, tin and mercury, with carbon explicitly excluded as a modern retrojection. On the normal received-text/lexicographical standard the clean, uncontested omissions remain tin and mercury.

